<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>

<!--
  elasticdms.wxs — the Windows installation package (MSI), WiX v4/v5 schema.

  ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
  NOT RUN ON WINDOWS. This file was written on a Mac and checked there only as XML (xmllint).
  No `wix build`, no `msiexec`, no `signtool` has run; the ICE checks of the Windows Installer
  run on Windows first. Expect rework at the first build — the likeliest places stand in
  README.md.

  By the way: XML comments do not tolerate two consecutive hyphens. Where command line switches
  are mentioned below they therefore appear without their prefix; what counts is the attribute
  underneath.
  ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

  WHY MSI AND NOT INNO SETUP OR MSIX
  The operator deploys over Intune AND over GPO. GPO software installation takes `.msi` only; an
  Inno Setup `.exe` could be delivered there through logon scripts alone — without assignment,
  without uninstallation, without inventory. Intune takes both, but for an MSI it runs the
  uninstallation itself as `msiexec /x {ProductCode}` and reads ProductCode and version for
  detection. MSIX/sparse is out twice over: ADR-D06 §7 forbids the WinRT registration path, and a
  sparse package does NOT bring the automatic sync root deregistration with it (that applies only
  to roots from `StorageProviderSyncRootManager.Register`); it would only buy a certificate
  obligation.

  WHICH WiX VERSION
  This file uses the v4 schema, which WiX 4, 5, 6 and 7 have in common (`Package` instead of
  `Product`, `Condition` as an attribute, `FileRef` instead of `FileKey`, `StandardDirectory`). It
  builds with every one of those versions. The WiX 3.14 preinstalled on `windows-2025` does NOT
  compile it. Which version is fetched stands in exactly one place: `WIX_VERSION` in
  `scripts/windows-package.ps1` (default 5.0.2). That is a legal question, not a technical one: up
  to 5.0.2 the NuGet package is under MS-RL, from 6.0.0 it carries OSMFEULA.txt with
  `requireLicenseAcceptance=true` — an "Open Source Maintenance Fee" for users above 10,000 US$
  annual revenue, and therefore paid use for a limited company. Whoever takes 6 or 7 decides that
  deliberately (README.md, section "The version of WiX is a legal question").

  TWO LANGUAGES, ONE PACKAGE AND A TRANSFORM BESIDE IT
  The four sentences a human reads no longer stand in this file. They stand in de-DE.wxl and
  en-US.wxl and are referenced here by name; `wix build` resolves every !(loc.…) before it writes
  the database, so a key that only one of the two catalogues knows is a WIX0102 at build time. That
  much was MEASURED on the development Mac (README.md, section "What can be proven without
  Windows").

  This file is built once per language. The first language becomes the MSI, every further one
  becomes a language transform (.mst) beside it. WHICH of the two shapes — two MSIs or one MSI plus
  a transform — costs the administrator less is decided, with the price of each, in
  scripts\windows-package.ps1 at $Cultures. Nothing of that decision is visible here: this file
  only has to build once per language for it, and it does.

  What is fixed here is the Codepage, 1252 for both catalogues. A language outside Latin-1 (932 for
  Japanese) may NOT be a transform on this package: a transform that changes the code page is an
  error condition of the Windows Installer which the applying side has to suppress by hand, and a
  suppressed error is not a localisation. Such a language gets a package of its own.

  THE GUIDs — ROLLED ONCE, NEVER CHANGED AGAIN
  The same discipline as `PROVIDER_GUID` in `crates/cfapi/src/sync_root.rs`: a changed GUID means
  "another product" or "another component" to Windows, and the old one stays behind as a corpse.

  GUID                                   What for              Consequence of a change
  ====================================== ===================== =======================================
  92B92D26-B56F-4151-95BC-EA80102776C4   UpgradeCode           MajorUpgrade no longer finds the old
                                                               version; both stand side by side in
                                                               the program list.
  4DCB2E4C-4EFA-48AC-A677-A7FFC39501FB   Component Program     The old elasticdms.exe is no longer
                                                               counted on uninstallation.
  3E01A719-B97E-4705-BE78-2FC3AF77E67C   Component Autostart   The old HKLM Run value stays behind
                                                               after uninstallation.
  7C5D0A83-16F2-4B1E-9E40-2B0B4E5F1A55   Component Environment The old EDMS variables stay behind
                                                               after uninstallation.

  The ProductCode is deliberately NOT fixed: it is rolled anew per build, and that is precisely
  what `MajorUpgrade` demands. Intune reads it out of the MSI for detection and for the
  uninstallation command. Who rolls it moved with the second language — see the guard below.

  THE LIFE CYCLE OF THE SYNC ROOT IS THE CORE OF THIS FILE
  The client registers its root with Win32 `CfRegisterSyncRoot` (ADR-D06 §7, unpackaged). Windows
  remembers roots per volume; one left behind jams every later installation. Hence the action
  `UnregisterSyncRoots` below — and hence it is NOT the only countermeasure: the client has to be
  allowed to take over an orphaned root of its own (open point in README.md), otherwise one machine
  that was switched off during the uninstallation is enough to stall every later installation.
-->

<Wix xmlns="http://wixtoolset.org/schemas/v4/wxs"
     xmlns:util="http://wixtoolset.org/schemas/v4/wxs/util">

  <!--
    Two values come from outside so that there is only one truth for each: the version from
    `[workspace.package] version` and the directory of the built `.exe`. If one is missing the
    build stops with a whole sentence instead of an empty attribute. A third, the ProductCode,
    follows below — it has a default and therefore no error.

    The names are those the build script passes. They are checked by a test
    (`crates/architecture-rules/tests/scripts.rs`), because a renamed variable fails nowhere else
    than on a Windows runner after a ten-minute build.
  -->
  <?ifndef Version?>
    <?error The version is missing. Call: wix build -d Version=0.1.0 -d BinDir=... ; scripts\windows-package.ps1 sets both itself from Cargo.toml.?>
  <?endif?>
  <?ifndef BinDir?>
    <?error The directory holding elasticdms.exe is missing. Call: wix build -d BinDir=target\x86_64-pc-windows-msvc\release ...?>
  <?endif?>

  <!--
    The ProductCode, unlike the two above, has a default: by hand `wix` rolls it itself, which is
    what the star means. The build script overrides it, and it has to.

    WHY: the language transform is the difference between two builds of this file, one per
    catalogue. A difference that also moved the ProductCode would not be a language transform but
    two products — two rows in Programs and Features, two detection rules in Intune, and a
    transform the Windows Installer refuses because its validation compares the product it was cut
    from. So the script rolls ONE GUID per run and hands the same one to every language. Per build a
    new one, exactly as before; per language the same one.
  -->
  <?ifndef ProductCode?>
    <?define ProductCode = *?>
  <?endif?>

  <Package
      Name="elasticdms"
      Manufacturer="Lotzer Digital UG (haftungsbeschränkt)"
      Version="$(var.Version)"
      UpgradeCode="92B92D26-B56F-4151-95BC-EA80102776C4"
      ProductCode="$(var.ProductCode)"
      Scope="perMachine"
      Language="!(loc.PackageLanguage)"
      Codepage="1252"
      InstallerVersion="500"
      Compressed="yes">

    <!--
      AllowSameVersionUpgrades, because MSI evaluates only three version fields (at most
      255.255.65535) and ignores the fourth: two builds of the same Cargo version are the same
      version to MSI, and without this switch MajorUpgrade would refuse every reinstallation after
      a rebuild.

      The sentence a human reads on a downgrade stands in the two .wxl catalogues, like every other
      end user text of this product.
    -->
    <MajorUpgrade
        AllowSameVersionUpgrades="yes"
        DowngradeErrorMessage="!(loc.DowngradeBlocked)" />

    <MediaTemplate EmbedCab="yes" />

    <!--
      The client is x64 only (target x86_64-pc-windows-msvc); the package is built with
      `-arch x64`. Without this condition a 32-bit Windows would get the installer's standard
      message instead of a sentence that says what is missing.

      NO condition on the Windows version: `VersionNT` reads 603 on every Windows from 8.1 on, and
      the installer does not see the build number (10.0.16299 for the Cloud Filter API). The check
      therefore belongs in the client (`edms_cfapi::platform_available`), which already does it —
      there with a sentence that names the reason.
    -->
    <Launch
        Condition="VersionNT64"
        Message="!(loc.NeedsSixtyFourBitWindows)" />

    <!-- There is only one feature set; a "Change" button would lead nowhere. -->
    <Property Id="ARPNOMODIFY" Value="1" />
    <Property Id="ARPCOMMENTS" Value="!(loc.ProductComments)" />

    <!--
      The icon of the row in "Programs and Features". Without ARPPRODUCTICON the row stays blank
      between the rows of every other product.

      THE ID IS A FILE NAME AND ENDS IN .ico ON PURPOSE. The Id is the Name column of the Icon
      table, and the Windows Installer writes that icon out to a file of exactly that name before
      the shell reads it; the shell goes by the extension. An Id "ProductIcon" would make an
      extensionless file out of it. NOT MEASURED — that is the documented behaviour of the Icon
      table, and nothing here has ever seen Programs and Features — but it costs nothing to spell
      the Id the way the documentation asks for.

      WHERE THE FILE COMES FROM. It is not in this repository: crates/app/build.rs draws it out of
      crates/app/src/icon.rs and puts it next to the built elasticdms.exe — that is the same
      directory BinDir names, the one the File element in the fragment below reads the program from.
      `wix build` has to be able to open it while it builds, so the order matters:

        scripts\windows-package.ps1 -Task build   cargo build, THEN wix build — the file is there.
        scripts\windows-package.ps1 -Task msi     packs an already built exe; the script checks for
                                                  the .ico beside it and stops with a sentence.

      Both workflows in .github\workflows call the first of the two (job "Package Windows
      (unsigned)" in ci.yml, job "Windows .msi" in release.yml), and both let that one script do
      the compiling. There is no path on which wix runs without a cargo build before it.
    -->
    <Icon Id="elasticdms.ico" SourceFile="$(var.BinDir)\elasticdms.ico" />
    <Property Id="ARPPRODUCTICON" Value="elasticdms.ico" />

    <!--
      The three mandatory variables of the client (README, section Configuration). They are public
      MSI properties (upper case) and can therefore be set on the command line:

        msiexec /i elasticdms-0.1.0.msi /qn ^
          EDMS_API_BASE=https://api.elasticdms.io ^
          EDMS_AUTH_BASE=https://auth.elasticdms.io ^
          EDMS_APP_BASE=https://app.elasticdms.io

      `Secure="yes"` enters them in SecureCustomProperties; without that they would not reach the
      deferred actions of a per-machine installation. Whoever prefers to set the variables over GPO
      preferences or Intune leaves them out — then the component "Environment" does not come into
      being and the client takes the values from the environment it finds.
    -->
    <Property Id="EDMS_API_BASE" Secure="yes" />
    <Property Id="EDMS_AUTH_BASE" Secure="yes" />
    <Property Id="EDMS_APP_BASE" Secure="yes" />

    <Feature Id="MainPart" Title="!(loc.FeatureTitle)" Level="1">
      <ComponentGroupRef Id="ProgramParts" />
    </Feature>

    <!-- ProgramFiles64Folder, not ProgramFiles6432Folder: the client is x64 only. -->
    <StandardDirectory Id="ProgramFiles64Folder">
      <Directory Id="INSTALLFOLDER" Name="elasticdms" />
    </StandardDirectory>

    <!--
      First stop, then deregister: `CfUnregisterSyncRoot` fails with
      ERROR_CLOUD_FILE_INVALID_REQUEST as long as a provider is CONNECTED to the root
      (`CfConnectSyncRoot`). If elasticdms.exe is still running in any session, a root would stay
      behind on every machine with a signed-in user.

      ElevatedCloseMessage, because the uninstallation runs as SYSTEM under Intune and GPO and the
      window message would otherwise not reach the user's session. TerminateProcess="0" ends the
      process with exit code 0 if it does not react to the message; with the folder client that is
      harmless: it writes nothing that an ending could tear apart (requirement 1, a reading
      mirror), and the session lies in the keychain, not in memory.

      NOT PROVEN: whether the message really reaches processes in OTHER sessions. If it does not,
      the cleanup action reports the root as left behind (README.md, residual risk).
    -->
    <util:CloseApplication
        Id="StopFolderClient"
        Target="elasticdms.exe"
        CloseMessage="yes"
        ElevatedCloseMessage="yes"
        TerminateProcess="0"
        RebootPrompt="no" />

    <!--
      The cleanup action. `FileRef` points at the installed file (in WiX v4/v5 the attribute is no
      longer called FileKey).

      Execute="deferred" with Impersonate="no" means SYSTEM in session 0: no user profile, no HKCU
      of the user, no keychain, no desktop. Impersonate="yes" would not help — it would imitate
      whoever started `msiexec`, and under Intune, SCCM and GPO that is SYSTEM as well. There is no
      MSI mechanism that runs from a per-machine uninstallation in the context of the signed-in
      user. That is why the switch below is a pure command line mode which touches none of it
      (crates/app/src/uninstall.rs).

      This is possible because `CfUnregisterSyncRoot`, according to the documentation, requires
      only WRITE_DATA or WRITE_DAC on the root folder — no user identity, no elevation. SYSTEM has
      both in every profile, and that is why this single action clears ALL profiles instead of just
      one.

      Return="ignore", because a failed cleanup would otherwise make the product unremovable. The
      price is named: a failure only shows up at the next installation. What stayed behind is
      written by the switch to its output; with `msiexec /x ... /l*v` it stands in the log.
    -->
    <CustomAction
        Id="UnregisterSyncRoots"
        FileRef="ElasticdmsExe"
        ExeCommand="--uninstall"
        Execute="deferred"
        Impersonate="no"
        Return="ignore" />

    <InstallExecuteSequence>
      <!--
        Before="RemoveFiles", because the .exe is gone afterwards.
        REMOVE="ALL": only on a real uninstallation, not when single parts are removed.
        NOT UPGRADINGPRODUCTCODE: on an update RemoveExistingProducts removes the old version —
        there the root shall precisely NOT be deregistered and the mirror NOT deleted, the new
        version takes over both.
      -->
      <Custom
          Action="UnregisterSyncRoots"
          Before="RemoveFiles"
          Condition="REMOVE=&quot;ALL&quot; AND NOT UPGRADINGPRODUCTCODE" />
    </InstallExecuteSequence>
  </Package>

  <Fragment>
    <!--
      The files are listed one by one, not harvested (no `heat`, no HarvestDirectory): a build over
      the whole workspace also puts `edms-mock.exe` into the same directory, and the test rig may
      never end up in a delivered artefact (README, crate layout). Whoever adds something here
      decides to do so.
    -->
    <ComponentGroup Id="ProgramParts" Directory="INSTALLFOLDER">

      <Component Id="Program" Guid="4DCB2E4C-4EFA-48AC-A677-A7FFC39501FB" Bitness="always64">
        <File Id="ElasticdmsExe" Source="$(var.BinDir)\elasticdms.exe" KeyPath="yes" />
      </Component>

      <!--
        Autostart per machine: one value under HKLM Run, all users, no Active Setup, no scheduled
        task.

        HKLM instead of HKCU: a per-machine package may not have an HKCU value as its KeyPath — at
        installation time there is only one user, and every further one would trigger a self-repair
        at their first sign-in (visible on a terminal server as a hang).

        Deliberately HKLM Run and not a scheduled task: the value appears in Task Manager under
        "Startup" and can be switched off by the user. For a READING mirror nothing speaks against
        letting them; a scheduled task would be more robust, would demand an uninstallation action
        of its own and would feel patronising. The startup folder under %ProgramData% would be out
        anyway: no binding to the component, and within reach of folder redirection and OneDrive
        profile backup.

        The value disappears by itself on uninstallation — unlike everything the client writes at
        runtime.
      -->
      <Component Id="Autostart" Guid="3E01A719-B97E-4705-BE78-2FC3AF77E67C" Bitness="always64">
        <RegistryValue
            Root="HKLM"
            Key="SOFTWARE\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\Run"
            Name="elasticdms"
            Type="string"
            Value="&quot;[INSTALLFOLDER]elasticdms.exe&quot;"
            KeyPath="yes" />
      </Component>

      <!--
        The three mandatory variables as system variables. Without them the client does not start
        but writes a sentence to the error output naming the missing variable
        (crates/app/src/setup.rs) — on a managed device nobody would see it.

        System="yes": the machine, not the user. Explorer, and with it the autostart, inherits the
        value at the next sign-in; a running Explorer does not see it (Windows distributes changed
        system variables only to processes that listen for WM_SETTINGCHANGE). After a first
        installation a sign-out and sign-in is therefore needed; with an Intune delivery before the
        first sign-in that goes unnoticed.

        Action="set" with Permanent="no": on uninstallation they disappear again.

        The component only comes into being if all three values stand on the command line. The
        KeyPath is a registry value because Environment rows cannot be one.
      -->
      <Component
          Id="Environment"
          Guid="7C5D0A83-16F2-4B1E-9E40-2B0B4E5F1A55"
          Bitness="always64"
          Condition="EDMS_API_BASE AND EDMS_AUTH_BASE AND EDMS_APP_BASE">
        <RegistryValue
            Root="HKLM"
            Key="SOFTWARE\Lotzer Digital\elasticdms"
            Name="EnvironmentSet"
            Type="integer"
            Value="1"
            KeyPath="yes" />
        <Environment Id="ApiBase" Name="EDMS_API_BASE" Value="[EDMS_API_BASE]"
                     Action="set" Part="all" System="yes" Permanent="no" />
        <Environment Id="AuthBase" Name="EDMS_AUTH_BASE" Value="[EDMS_AUTH_BASE]"
                     Action="set" Part="all" System="yes" Permanent="no" />
        <Environment Id="AppBase" Name="EDMS_APP_BASE" Value="[EDMS_APP_BASE]"
                     Action="set" Part="all" System="yes" Permanent="no" />
      </Component>

    </ComponentGroup>
  </Fragment>
</Wix>
